From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DannyBaanks <danny@localhost>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 00:42:58 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Linux: the host's service layer builds and runs on aarch64
 Linux

The portable C of dm/host and dm/gfx is unchanged in behaviour; what Linux needs is
a small stand-in layer under dm/host/linux (dm/host/Makefile.linux builds the same
sources against it):

  include/       the Mach interfaces the host takes from the kernel, over mmap and
                 /proc/self/mem; the CoreFoundation and CommonCrypto calls it makes;
                 GCD, the quality-of-service classes and the scheduler interfaces;
                 the section lookup of the host's own tables
  linux_compat.c their implementations, with the parts that have no Linux equivalent
                 reported instead of pretended
  linux_dispatch.c, linux_blocks.c   GCD over threads, and the block runtime it needs
  front_linux.c  the front end: the guest runs on the calling thread, no window
  linux_gfx_stub.c   the Metal seam's interface, answered with nothing: the host runs,
                 nothing is drawn, and the first call that would have drawn says so
  linux_media_stub.c  audio and the movie decoders, missing until their device layer
  asm_elf.py     the Mach-O assembly converted to ELF (entry.S, the NVN thunks); the
                 macOS build keeps the files it always had

What the host does on Linux now: it boots, finds its HLE table, reads the game profile
and the module directory, and its crash dump reads a thread's stack. It stops where the
game's own files start (there is no game on this machine's checkouts).

The audio and movie files need Apple's AudioToolbox and VideoToolbox and do not build;
the loader's unimplemented stubs cover their imports.

make -f dm/host/Makefile.linux GAME=<id> test passes (compat memory, sections, clock,
mappings, threads, the register view of a signal handler, the queue's order).
Upstream-Status: fork-only (the Mac is the upstream platform; a Linux build is this fork's work)
Reason: The portable C of dm/host and dm/gfx is unchanged: Linux only needs a stand-in layer for the platform interfaces the host takes from Apple, built against by dm/host/Makefile.linux. It keeps the diff to the shared sources to the minimum and reports what cannot be reproduced instead of pretending.
Issue: LNPANDY/NX2Apple#214

