+//
+// The host's portable service layer talks to the graphics backend through two C interfaces
+// that the Metal backend (nvn_mtl.m) answers: the memory pools and the submission
+// (dm/gfx/nvn/nvn_mtl.h), and the objects, the timing and the counters
+// (dm/gfx/nvn/nvn_obj.h and the headers it includes). On Linux there is no Metal yet, so
+// this file answers that whole interface with the smallest thing that keeps the host
+// running: the pools are the game's own memory, a submission completes as soon as it is
+// made, a wait returns at once, nothing is drawn, and every counter reads zero.
+//
+// The Vulkan backend (the real work of the port) takes the same interface, in its own
+// nvn_vk.h; until it exists this stub keeps the host's core buildable and testable. What
+// the stub deliberately does NOT do is pretend: the first call that would have drawn says
+// so in the log, and the counters stay zero so no claim about the graphics is made.
